{% sec 1, "Two Ways to Run a Fund", "How is a hedge fund organised inside?", "Two ways to run a fund" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every hedge fund has two halves. The <strong>front office</strong> makes the money: the people who find ideas, decide on trades and place them. The <strong>business side</strong> keeps everything safe and running: risk, operations, compliance, technology and the people who look after investors.</p>
    <p>How the front office is arranged depends on which of two models the fund follows. Think of a restaurant with one famous head chef who decides every dish, compared with a food hall where dozens of independent stalls share one building, one kitchen supply and one set of rules.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A {% gl "single-manager fund" %}single-manager fund{% endgl %} is the restaurant. One founder, the chief investment officer, makes the final call on every position. The team is small (often \(10\) to \(30\) people) and works closely together. Junior analysts describe it as an apprenticeship: you watch one experienced investor think, every day.</li>
      <li>A {% gl "multi-strategy platform" %}multi-strategy platform{% endgl %}, nicknamed a "pod shop", is the food hall. The firm hands money to dozens or hundreds of independent {% gl "portfolio manager" %}portfolio managers{% endgl %}, each running their own small team (a pod). The firm provides the building: technology, data, risk, operations. Citadel and Millennium are the best-known examples.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The defining rule of a pod shop is the stop-loss on each PM. If a PM's book falls by a set amount, often \(5\%\) to \(10\%\), the risk team cuts it down, and past a certain point the PM loses their money or their job. Because hundreds of pods are making different bets, the firm's overall returns come out much smoother than any single pod's.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>The two shapes</h3><p>Left: one decision-maker. Right: many independent pods sharing one platform.</p></div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-orgs" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="A single-manager fund with one chief investment officer above analysts and traders, next to a platform with a central risk team above many independent PM pods."></svg>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Single-manager funds give you a broad apprenticeship under one investor. Platforms give you a clear, numbers-driven route to running money, with much less job security.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "The Roles in a Fund", "Who does what inside a hedge fund?", "Roles in a fund" %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>In one line</th><th>What they're judged on</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Analyst</td><td>Finds ideas where the market is wrong, and proves it</td><td>The quality of their ideas and research</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Portfolio manager (PM)</td><td>Decides what goes in the portfolio, how big, and when to cut</td><td>The fund's overall profit and risk</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trader</td><td>Buys and sells for the PM as cheaply and quietly as possible, and reports what's happening in the market</td><td>Execution costs and the quality of their market information</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Risk team</td><td>Watches every position independently and enforces the limits</td><td>Making sure nothing blows up the fund</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operations</td><td>Books, settles and reconciles every trade, and works out what the fund is worth</td><td>Accuracy: mistakes here cost real money</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>Keeps the fund inside the law, especially on inside information</td><td>No breaches</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Investor relations</td><td>Raises money and keeps investors informed</td><td>Money raised and kept</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The analyst: "find me where the market is wrong"</h3>
    <p>An analyst's job comes down to one request from the PM: find ideas where the market is wrong, and prove it. Describing a great company isn't an idea. A real idea explains why the price is wrong, what will make it right (the {% gl "catalyst" %}catalyst{% endgl %}), how much could be made or lost, and what evidence would prove the idea wrong.</p>
    <p>The best ideas rest on {% gl "variant perception" %}variant perception{% endgl %}: a specific view that differs from the consensus, backed by evidence, with a reason the market will come round to it. That's different from being contrarian, which is just disagreeing with the crowd.</p>
    <h3>The PM: from "is this a good idea?" to "does it fit?"</h3>
    <p>An analyst asks whether an idea is good. A PM asks how it fits with everything else they own, how big to make it, and when to cut it if it goes wrong. Many great analysts struggle as PMs, because the conviction that makes a good analyst can stop them cutting a losing position.</p>
    <h3>The risk team: independent on purpose</h3>
    <p>The people taking risk are the worst people to judge it, because everyone underestimates the risk in their own ideas. So at a well-run fund, the risk team reports to the top of the firm, not to the PMs, and can force cuts even when a PM disagrees. Most hedge fund disasters happened when that independence broke down.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Life on a Bank Trading Desk", "What does the job I'm aiming for actually look like?", "Life on a bank desk" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark's lesson is about the buy side. An FX or commodities sales and trading job is on the sell side, in a bank's markets division, and it's a different life.</p>
    <h3>Who sits on the desk</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sales</td><td>Look after clients (hedge funds, fund managers, companies, central banks), bring them trade ideas, and take their orders</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trader</td><td>Makes the prices clients trade on and manages the risk the desk is left holding. Judged on their own profit and loss.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Structurer</td><td>Designs tailored products for clients, like a currency hedge shaped to a company's exact payments</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strats (desk quants)</td><td>Build pricing models and the systems that quote prices automatically</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strategist / research</td><td>Write the bank's market views, which sales use with clients</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle office and risk</td><td>Check the desk's positions, profit and loss and limits every day</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A bank trader is different from a hedge fund trader. A hedge fund trader carries out someone else's decisions as cheaply as possible. A bank trader makes prices for clients and carries the risk of what's left over, so their own numbers are what they're judged on.</p>
    <h3>A day on an FX desk</h3>
    <p>FX never closes during the week, so banks pass their positions around the world: Asia hands the book to London in the morning, and London hands it to New York in the afternoon. London is the biggest FX trading centre in the world, so the London desk sits in the middle of the action.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Time (UK)</th><th>What's happening</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>6:30</td><td>Read the Asia desk's handover notes: what moved overnight, what the book looks like</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7:00</td><td>Morning meeting: the day's data releases, central bank events, client flows to expect</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7:30–8:00</td><td>London opens and volumes jump. Quote prices, manage the risk</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Morning</td><td>UK and European data releases. Sales call clients with ideas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>13:30</td><td>The big US data (jobs, inflation) usually lands now. Often the busiest minutes of the day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16:00</td><td>The London "fix": a benchmark rate many funds trade at, so lots of orders arrive at once</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Evening</td><td>Hand the book to New York, write up the day, read for tomorrow</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Careers and Pay", "How do careers progress, and how does pay work?", "Careers and pay" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The ladders</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>At a bank:</strong> Analyst, then Associate, then Vice President (VP), then Director, then Managing Director (MD). Each step takes a few years.</li>
      <li><strong>At a hedge fund:</strong> Junior analyst, then analyst, then senior analyst, then sector head or deputy PM, then portfolio manager. There's no fixed timetable: it depends almost entirely on how good your ideas turn out to be.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>People move between them all the time. Two years at a bank is a common stepping stone into private equity or a hedge fund. Bank traders often move to the buy side, and sell-side analysts often become buy-side analysts.</p>
    <h3>How pay works</h3>
    <p>Pay is a base salary plus a bonus, and the bonus is where the big differences are. At a bank it comes from the bank's overall results and your own reviews. At a hedge fund it's tied much more directly to profits, so it can be huge in a good year and close to nothing in a bad one. Rough US figures from the lesson: junior roles at most firms pay around \(\$150\text{k}\) to \(\$350\text{k}\) in total in the first years. A platform PM is paid \(15\%\) to \(25\%\) of the profit they make, which can be millions, or zero.</p>
    <p>Two details matter more than they look:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>{% gl "deferred compensation" %}Deferred bonus{% endgl %}.</strong> Many firms pay part of the bonus later, over two to four years, and you lose it if you leave early. These are called golden handcuffs.</li>
      <li><strong>The {% gl "high-water mark" %}high-water mark{% endgl %}.</strong> After a losing year, a PM has to win the losses back before they get paid on profits again. Try it below.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>A PM's pay over three years</h3><p>A platform PM runs \(\$750\) million and is paid \(20\%\) of profits, with a high-water mark. Set the profit or loss for each year.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="y1">Year 1</label><input type="range" id="y1" min="-90" max="120" step="5" value="60"><output id="y1-out" for="y1">$60m</output></div>
