{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "What Interviewers Look For", "What are they actually testing?", "What interviewers want" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Intelligence is the entry ticket, not the differentiator. Across the industry, interviewers are testing for a handful of things:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>They want to see</th><th>How they test it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>You have real views</td><td>"Pitch me a trade." "What's the market getting wrong?"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>You think about risk, not just reward</td><td>"What's the downside? What would make you change your mind? Where would you cut it?"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>You know the markets you want to work in</td><td>"Where's EUR/USD? What moved it this week? What's the next big event?"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Numbers come easily to you</td><td>Mental maths, probability puzzles, quick estimates (especially for trading roles)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>You communicate clearly under pressure</td><td>Live pushback on your pitch; explaining something complex simply</td></tr>
        <tr><td>You're honest about mistakes</td><td>"Tell me about a time you were wrong."</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The analysts who get promoted fastest share five habits: they only pitch ideas with genuine variant perception, they do their own research rather than repeating others', they admit mistakes quickly, they stay precise under pressure, and they follow a repeatable process instead of waiting for inspiration. The same habits come across in an interview, and a hub like this one is evidence of the last one.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Portfolio manager</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The person who decides what goes in a portfolio, how big, and when to cut. Usually called the PM.</p><p class="eg">"Pitch it to the PM at the morning meeting."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Single-manager fund</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A hedge fund where one founder makes the final call on every position.</p><p class="eg">Pershing Square, Lone Pine.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Multi-strategy platform</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A firm that hands money to many independent PM teams (pods), with central risk limits. Nicknamed a pod shop.</p><p class="eg">Citadel, Millennium, Point72.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Variant perception</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A specific, evidence-backed view that differs from the consensus, with a reason the market will come round to it.</p><p class="eg">"What's your variant perception here?"</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Catalyst</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The event that will make the market see what you see, and move the price.</p><p class="eg">Results day, a central bank meeting, a court ruling.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Drawdown</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How far a portfolio has fallen from its highest point.</p><p class="eg">"He's in a \(6\%\) drawdown, so risk is cutting his gross."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>High-water mark</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The previous peak. After losses, a manager must get back above it before earning performance pay again.</p><p class="eg">"She's below her high-water mark, so no payout this year."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Deferred compensation</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Part of a bonus paid over the next few years, lost if you leave early. The golden handcuffs.</p><p class="eg">"Half my bonus vests over three years."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where these roles turn up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: think like a PM, not just an analyst</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The PM habits in this note are the ones that matter for any trader: decide in advance where you'll cut, size positions so one mistake can't sink you, and respect hard drawdown limits. Prop firm challenges use exactly the same idea as a pod shop's stop-loss: lose a set amount and the account is gone. The Trading topic goes deeper into position sizing and risk.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: the same roles, different assets</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A property fund has the same shape: analysts who find and check deals, a decision-maker who decides what to buy and how much to borrow, operations and investor relations. When it comes to partnering with a real estate specialist, knowing who does what on their side makes the conversation much easier.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: desks in Lagos</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Nigerian banks have treasury and markets desks too, trading naira, government bonds and money markets, with the same split of sales, trading and risk. A fintech that serves businesses' FX needs is, in effect, building a digital version of the sales desk: understanding what a client needs and getting them a fair price quickly.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
