{% sec 8, "The Maths Underneath", "How do payouts, exposure and bet sizes work?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Payout with a high-water mark</h3>
    <p>Keep a running total of losses still to win back, \(D\) (start at \(0\)). Each year, with profit or loss \(P\) and payout rate \(p\):</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">One year's payout</span>\[ \text{payout} = p \times \max(0,\ P - D) \qquad D_{\text{next}} = \max(0,\ D - P) \]</div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 1</span>
    <h3>\(20\%\) payout. Year 1: \(+\$60\text{m}\). Year 2: \(-\$30\text{m}\). Year 3: \(+\$45\text{m}\).</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Year 1: \(D = 0\), payout \(= 0.2 \times 60 = \$12\text{m}\).</li>
      <li>Year 2: a loss, so payout \(= 0\) and \(D\) becomes \(\$30\text{m}\).</li>
      <li>Year 3: payout \(= 0.2 \times (45 - 30) = \$3\text{m}\), and \(D\) is back to \(0\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Total \(\$15\text{m}\). Without the high-water mark it would have been \(\$21\text{m}\). These are the starting settings of the chart in section 4.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Gross and net exposure</h3>
    <p>A fund that bets on some shares rising (longs) and others falling (shorts) measures its size two ways, as a percentage of its own money:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Exposure</span>\[ \text{Gross} = \frac{\text{longs} + \text{shorts}}{\text{fund size}} \qquad \text{Net} = \frac{\text{longs} - \text{shorts}}{\text{fund size}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A fund with \(100\%\) long and \(50\%\) short has gross \(150\%\) (it's using borrowed money) and net \(50\%\) (it captures roughly half of a market move).</p>
    <h3>How big to bet: the Kelly criterion</h3>
    <p>For a bet that wins with probability \(q\) and pays \(b\) times your stake (losing your stake otherwise), the size that grows your money fastest over time is:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Kelly fraction</span>\[ f = \frac{q\,b - (1 - q)}{b} \]</div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 2</span>
    <h3>An idea wins \(60\%\) of the time and pays the same as it risks (\(b = 1\)).</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\(f = \frac{0.6 \times 1 - 0.4}{1} = 0.2\), so Kelly says bet \(20\%\).</li>
      <li>Real PMs use a fraction of Kelly, often a quarter to a half, because they never know the true probability: \(5\%\) to \(10\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Betting more than your real edge justifies is one of the fastest ways to lose money, even with good ideas.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer. These double as interview questions." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>A \(\$2\) billion fund has \(40\) positions. The biggest is \(8\%\), and the top \(10\) make up \(55\%\). The PM wants to add a new idea at \(6\%\). What should the risk team ask?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The fund is already concentrated: over half its money is in ten names. Risk would ask whether the new idea is correlated with the big existing positions (same sector, same economic driver), how quickly a \(\$120\) million position could be sold in a crisis, what it does to sector and factor limits, and whether something else should be trimmed to make room.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Single-manager fund or platform for a first job? Make the case for each.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Single-manager: you see the whole portfolio and learn how one great investor thinks, but your career depends on that person, and becoming a PM usually means leaving.</span><span>Platform: a clear, measurable route to running money and lots of resources, but you see a narrow slice and pods can be shut down quickly. Many people suggest starting where you'll learn most broadly, then moving.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>Why must the risk team be independent of the people investing?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>People underestimate the risk in their own positions: they anchor on their thesis and hate admitting losses. An independent team with power to force cuts protects the fund from that. When risk teams were overruled or reported to the investors, losses were allowed to grow, as in the collapse of Long-Term Capital Management in 1998.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A PM has ideas at conviction levels low, moderate, high and highest. Roughly how big should each be, and what else limits the sizes?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Roughly \(1\)–\(2\%\), \(2\)–\(4\%\), \(4\)–\(7\%\) and \(7\)–\(10\%\) of the fund. But the sizes also depend on how easily each can be sold (liquidity), whether the ideas move together (correlation), sector limits, and whether the downside is limited.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>A fund is \(130\%\) long and \(70\%\) short. What are its gross and net exposure?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Gross \(= 130 + 70 = 200\%\). Net \(= 130 - 70 = 60\%\).</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
